<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chormaqan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1241</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Chormagan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chormaqan was a Mongol general who led the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> Ögedei. Sent in 1230 against the Khwarazmian ruler Jalal al-Din, he drove him out of north-western Iran, and from 1236 his armies overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, forcing Queen Rusudan to submit. He governed the region from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughan-plain">Mughan plain</a> until illness ended his command around 1241.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chormaqan, also called Chormagan or Chormaqan Noyan, belonged to the Sunud tribe and served in the keshik, the imperial guard of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogedei-khan">Ögedei Khan</a>. He is mentioned many times in The Secret History of the Mongols. As a quiver bearer he is often called Qurchi in the sources. He probably took part in the Mongol campaigns in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and later in the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russian steppes</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the winter of 1230 Ögedei sent him to renew the Mongol conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, which had stalled after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s assault on the Khwarezmid Empire between 1218 and 1223. His army is said to have numbered some 30,000 to 50,000 men, and the small Khwarezmid band under Jalal ad-Din was swept away at its approach. From the steppes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, his campaigns in the mid-1230s made the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and the Armenian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> vassals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, perhaps to secure communications for the westward attack under Batu that soon followed.</p><p>Local rulers came over to him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baraq-hajib">Buraq Hajib</a> of the Qutlugkhanids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> sent his son Rukn al-Din Mubarak Khwaja to Ögedei's court, and the Salghurid atabeg Abu Bakr sent his brother or nephew Tahamtan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>. Isfahan refused to submit, and he besieged it in 1236. After the Georgian and Armenian lords submitted, he divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> into eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumens</a>. His authority weakened in 1239, when Ögedei gave fiscal administration to Chinqai and Korguz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Chormaqan died around 1241. His wife Altani, who held considerable influence in his ordo, proposed his lieutenant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> as his successor, and Baiju was appointed. His son Shiramun later served the Il-khans Hulagu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a>, while another son, Bora, was executed under Hulagu. His daughter Esukan married King David VII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and the Armenian prince Hasan Jalal offered him his daughter Ruzanna in marriage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chormaqan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
